---
import GuideActions from '../GuideActions.astro';
import FAQ from '../FAQ.astro';
import LicensedImage from '../primitives/LicensedImage.astro';
import GuideBlock from '../model-guide/GuideBlock.astro';
import Photo from '../model-guide/Photo.astro';
import joePortrait from '../../assets/images/fender-sn/about-me-joe-1.jpg';
import RecordsTable from './RecordsTable.astro';
import CompareCards from './CompareCards.astro';
import ResaleTable from './ResaleTable.astro';
import kitCss from '../model-guide/model-guide.css?url';
import recordsCss from './records.css?url';
import {ranked,byId,hero,lastVerified,money,date,acousticRecord,biggestResale,bassHighs,lesPaulRecord,ordinaryModels,noCelebrity,ace,privateSales,faqs,titleCase} from '../../data/auction-records';
const black=ranked[0];
const sections = [
 {id:'record',nav:'The Record',title:'The Most Expensive Guitar Ever Sold',html:`
 <p>David Gilmour's Black Strat brought ${money(black.priceUsd)} at ${black.house}. The price beat the top of its published estimate by ${(black.priceUsd/black.estimateUsd!.high).toFixed(1)} times, including buyer's premium. It also comfortably exceeded its earlier public sale. Both transactions belong to the same guitar, so they appear together in the resale table instead of occupying separate places in the ranking.</p>
 <p>The auction catalog describes a Fender Stratocaster assembled from different periods. Its connection to Gilmour's work with Pink Floyd, including <em>The Dark Side Of The Moon</em>, <em>Wish You Were Here</em> and <em>The Wall</em>, is what gives the object its identity. Changing components during a musician's working life is part of that documented story. The result isn't a new price for an untouched production Stratocaster.</p>
 <p>That's why there's no ordinary-value card for the Black Strat. Choosing a factory year from its body and looking up a stock guitar would suggest a precision the comparison doesn't have. A collector bidding on this instrument is bidding on its traceable use, its recognizable identity and its place in the music. An owner of another black Strat needs a model and condition appraisal.</p>`},
 {id:'top-sales',nav:'All Results',title:'Every Guitar Sold For $1 Million Or More',html:`
 <p>The table contains ${ranked.length} distinct guitars with verified public auction results above the threshold. It ranks their latest verified sales by the amount paid at the time, including buyer's premium. A widely repeated figure doesn't qualify on repetition alone. Results we couldn't substantiate to that standard are left out, so this is a verified register rather than a claim that every reported sale has been proved.</p>
 <p>Use the price buttons to change the order, or narrow the list by instrument type and brand. The rank beside each guitar always refers to the original nominal-price order. Filtering to Martin, for example, preserves the guitar's place in the overall list instead of quietly assigning it a new rank. Bass results appear in the type section because the verified basses fall below the main table's cutoff.</p>
 <p>Inflation answers a different question from the headline record. It estimates the purchasing power of money paid in an earlier sale; it doesn't predict what the guitar would bring today. Auction estimates also deserve care. They usually describe an expected hammer price, while the reported result includes premium. Read the estimate multiple as a comparison with the catalog's expectation, not as a clean measure of investment performance.</p>`},
 {id:'ordinary-value',nav:'Famous Vs. Ordinary',title:'Famous Guitar Vs. An Ordinary One',html:`
 <p>The ordinary guitar has its own market. Start with the model and production period, then check the finish, components, repairs and condition. The cards below use the figures on Joe's current value pages. They don't divide an auction result by a generic price scraped from an advertisement, and they don't assume that every example of a model deserves the top of its range.</p>
 <p>Cobain's Competition Mustang makes the distinction easy to see. Joe's Mustang guide already explains that the famous guitar's value doesn't transfer to another Mustang, even one in the same color. Its comparison here uses the guide's Competition asking range. An asking price is a seller's proposal, not proof that a buyer completed a transaction at that amount. We keep that label visible beside the auction result.</p>
 <p>The SG comparison needs another qualification. The Fool's painted finish is part of its identity as Clapton's instrument. Joe's production-guitar range assumes original finish. Comparing them shows the scale of the celebrity result, but it doesn't suggest that repainting an ordinary SG creates the same value. Harrison's SG shares the model and year benchmark, while retaining its own separate ownership history.</p>
 <p>Some comparisons are deliberately incomplete. Joe doesn't publish a matching figure for Cobain's <a href="/post/martin-d18e-vs-d28e-authentication-guide/">D-18E</a>, Clapton's <a href="/prewar-martin-guitar-guide/">prewar Martin</a> <a href="/post/martin-0-00-000-history-authentication-value-guide/">000-42</a>, or Lennon's <a href="/vintage-gibson-acoustic-value-guide/">J-160E</a>. The <a href="/post/gretsch-6120-history-value/">Gretsch 6120 guide</a> labels its relevant double-cutaway figure for a neighboring year. Those gaps call for an appraisal, not an invented range. Custom Irwin guitars, Van Halen's Kramers and other individually modified celebrity instruments also lack a straightforward ordinary equivalent.</p>`},
 {id:'by-type',nav:'By Type',title:'Most Expensive Electric, Acoustic And Bass Guitars',html:`
 <p>The Black Strat leads electrics, while Cobain's Martin leads acoustics. An acoustic fitted with pickups still belongs with acoustics here; the presence of electronics alone doesn't turn a wooden acoustic body into a solidbody electric. The lists separate those categories so a reader interested in Martins doesn't have to work through every electric result to find the relevant benchmark.</p>
 <p>Bass has a separate scale. The highest verified result here is McCartney's Yamaha at ${money(bassHighs[0].priceUsd)}, including the premium published by Van Eaton Galleries. Its later sale was lower. For these type lists, we show each instrument's highest verified public result, which makes that historical bass record visible. The main million-dollar table uses latest results, and the resale table explains the difference.</p>
 <p>We could verify ${bassHighs.length} basses to the sourcing standard used here, so the bass list stops there. It isn't padded with estimates, private offers or an instrument's insurance valuation. A bass doesn't become less collectible because the category record is below the guitar headlines. Its maker, period, originality, playing condition and documented use still determine which comparison is useful for its owner.</p>`},
 {id:'by-brand',nav:'By Brand',title:'Records By Brand',html:`
 <p>The brand table is a shortcut to the highest verified public result for each maker, with Les Paul separated because it's a model family within Gibson. A Gibson record and a Les Paul record can belong to different guitars. The Fool leads the Gibson results here, while Beck's Oxblood holds the Les Paul distinction identified by Christie's. Neither result resets the ordinary vintage Gibson price tables.</p>
 <p>Oxblood is a particularly poor candidate for an all-original Goldtop comparison. Christie's describes alterations to its finish, neck and pickup configuration. Those changes belong in the description of Beck's specific instrument. An ordinary modified Les Paul has to be assessed on its own evidence; it doesn't inherit the value of another player's modifications simply because the work looks similar.</p>
 <p>Ace Frehley's ${ace.guitar} is no longer just an upcoming-auction headline. ${ace.reportedBy} reported a ${money(ace.priceUsd)} result from ${ace.house}' Music Icons sale held ${ace.saleWindow}. That reported result is below this table's threshold. It isn't included in the verified million-dollar ranking or treated as a new Les Paul record.</p>
 <p>Follow the linked value guide when the question concerns a guitar you own. A brand name alone leaves too much unresolved: an early production model, a later reissue and a signature edition can share a logo while belonging to different markets. The guide is the starting point for identification. Clear photographs and an inspection establish whether its original-condition figures apply.</p>`},
 {id:'sold-twice',nav:'Sold Twice',title:'Guitars That Sold Twice',html:`
 <p>The same guitar can return to auction with a very different result. Here, the multiple is the latest premium-inclusive price divided by the earlier premium-inclusive price. It isn't an annual rate of return, and it doesn't account for a seller's commission, storage, insurance, taxes or other ownership costs. Those expenses can matter even when the headline price rises.</p>
 <p>The Black Strat and Gilmour's D-35 moved from a charity sale into the Irsay collection and later returned to the public auction market. Clapton's Martin also has a documented earlier charity result. Those different sale settings belong alongside the dates. They give the reader more context than a percentage increase standing alone, but they don't tell us exactly how much any bidder's motivation affected the final price.</p>
 <p>McCartney's bass provides the counterexample to a page full of rising records. Its latest result is lower than its earlier charity-auction total. A famous name doesn't guarantee that each subsequent auction will exceed the last. Nor should an unverified earlier figure be slipped into the calculation because it makes a more dramatic story. The table only pairs transactions where the identity of the instrument and the earlier result can be traced.</p>`},
 {id:'no-famous-owner',nav:'No Famous Owner',title:'The Most Valuable Guitars With No Famous Owner',html:`
 <p>A guitar can have substantial value without a celebrity connection. Joe's production-model ranges below describe that market: desirable instruments evaluated for what they are, how much original material remains and how well they have survived. These are guide ranges, not auction records or offers to buy. An individual guitar can sit outside a published range when its condition or specification calls for separate assessment.</p>
 <p>The Sunburst Les Paul row is the clearest example. The plus sign on Joe's upper figure is intentional. It acknowledges an open upper end without promising that any guitar bearing the same model name will reach it. The <a href="/post/1958-gibson-les-paul-standard-guide/">earliest Sunbursts</a>, <a href="/post/1959-gibson-les-paul-standard-guide/">the following year's guitars</a> and <a href="/post/1960-gibson-les-paul-standard-guide/">the final original-era examples</a> need their own identification work. A replacement pickup or a repaired neck doesn't disappear because the model is desirable.</p>
 <p>There is also a separate public-auction benchmark. Heritage sold a ${noCelebrity.guitar} for ${money(noCelebrity.priceUsd)} on ${date(noCelebrity.saleDate,true)} and described it as a world auction record for a vintage guitar without celebrity provenance. The house emphasized preservation and originality. That is a result for one documented instrument, not a replacement for Joe's range and not proof that every comparable-looking guitar should be priced the same way.</p>
 <p>Original finish, correct parts, a coherent production date and a useful ownership file give an appraiser something concrete to evaluate. Even without a famous owner, an old receipt or a clear family photograph can help explain a guitar's history. Keep that material with the case. The goal is to establish what survives and what changed, then choose the right comparison. The <a href="/post/martin-d-45-value-guide/">D-45 guide</a> doesn't supply a prewar figure for this table, so that row is omitted.</p>`},
 {id:'private-sales',nav:'Private Sales',title:'Private And Unconfirmed Sales',html:`
 <p>Reported private deals belong outside a public-auction ranking. They may involve important guitars, but the available number can be a seller's asking price, an estimate repeated later as a sale price, or a transaction amount the parties never confirmed. A familiar story doesn't resolve which of those it is.</p>
 <p>The examples below are attributed reports, not verified prices. Neither enters the count, the inflation calculation or a brand record. We also avoid using a later owner's acquisition to prove the amount paid in an earlier transaction. Each transfer would need its own evidence.</p>
 <p>An upcoming auction is different again. Before bidding ends, an estimate describes an expectation. Afterward, a hammer amount still needs its buyer's premium before it can be compared with the totals on this page. If a guitar fails to sell or changes hands privately after the auction, that outcome shouldn't be silently converted into a public result. This distinction keeps a reported deal from displacing a documented record.</p>`},
 {id:'why',nav:'Why The Gap',title:'What Makes A Famous Guitar Worth Millions',html:`
 <p>A celebrity premium needs evidence connecting this physical guitar to the musician and the claimed use. The model name is the beginning of that work. A serial number, an old photograph, repair records and a chain of ownership have to agree closely enough to support the story being sold. A signature by itself doesn't establish that a guitar was used to record an album.</p>
 <p>Photo matching can connect marks and modifications to a particular moment. A distinctive patch of wear, a changed control or an unusual repair may be visible in stage footage or a studio photograph. The match needs to be specific enough to distinguish the instrument from another similar guitar. A color match alone leaves plenty of room for error, especially when a player used several instruments from the same maker.</p>
 <p>The paperwork matters because the guitar has often passed through other hands. A direct transfer, an auction catalog, correspondence and dated photographs can help account for those changes in ownership. Read what each document actually establishes. A letter confirming that someone owned the guitar doesn't necessarily prove a claim about which recording it appeared on. A later recollection deserves a different description from a contemporary photograph.</p>
 <p>Condition works differently in that context. A documented modification made during an artist's use can help identify the famous instrument. The same modification on an ordinary guitar is assessed for its effect on originality and function. You can't transfer the celebrity exception to a production guitar whose ownership story is unproved. That's especially relevant when a guitar has been refinished or routed for different pickups.</p>
 <p>For ordinary examples, start with <a href="/what-lowers-vintage-guitar-value/">the changes that lower vintage guitar value</a>. If the finish has been redone, use the <a href="/how-much-does-a-refinish-hurt-guitar-value/">refinish guide</a> to understand the inspection questions. Don't polish, disassemble or replace parts just to make photographs look tidier. Show the condition as it is, and disclose repairs you know about.</p>
 <p>A serial is useful evidence, but it isn't a complete authentication. <a href="/post/what-a-serial-number-cant-tell-you/">What a serial number can't tell you</a> explains why the rest of the instrument has to agree. Joe's <a href="/post/one-owner-1965-fender-stratocaster/">one-owner Stratocaster story</a> shows the kind of ordinary ownership context worth preserving. A family guitar doesn't need a stadium performance in its past to deserve a careful, documented appraisal.</p>`},
 {id:'yours',nav:'Your Guitar',title:'Could Your Guitar Be Valuable?',html:`
 <p>Yes, but the auction headlines aren't the place to choose its price. Start with identification. A maker's name, a model, an approximate year and a finish description narrow the search. Then separate what you know from what you've been told. An inherited guitar can come with a perfectly sincere family story that still needs supporting photographs or paperwork.</p>
 <p>If the model isn't clear, use <a href="/what-guitar-do-i-have/">the guitar identification guide</a>. If you've just opened an old case, <a href="/found-an-old-guitar/">the guide to finding an old guitar</a> explains what to document before making changes. Photograph the front and back, both sides of the headstock, the visible serial and any damage. Keep the case, loose parts, receipts and letters together.</p>
 <p>For a first value comparison, use the <a href="/vintage-fender-value-guide/">Fender value guide</a>, <a href="/vintage-gibson-value-guide/">Gibson value guide</a>, <a href="/vintage-gibson-acoustic-value-guide/">Gibson acoustic guide</a> or <a href="/martin-d-28-d-18-d-45-dreadnought-value-guide/">Martin dreadnought guide</a>. A <a href="/vintage-fender-stratocaster-value-guide/1964/">Stratocaster year guide</a> is more useful than the price of a famous player's differently configured guitar. Read the condition assumptions beside the figures before deciding that a range applies.</p>
 <p>Then send the photographs for a free appraisal. Include the changes you know about and say when a detail is uncertain. A replaced tuner, an old neck repair or an unusual finish can be evaluated; concealing it only makes the first assessment less useful. The appraisal should explain which details support the value and which still need inspection. It shouldn't depend on attaching somebody else's celebrity auction result to your guitar.</p>`},
];
const brandLinks:Record<string,string>={Fender:'/vintage-fender-value-guide/',Gibson:'/vintage-gibson-value-guide/',Martin:'/martin-d-28-d-18-d-45-dreadnought-value-guide/',Gretsch:'/post/gretsch-6120-history-value/',Rickenbacker:'/post/rickenbacker-325-value-guide/',Other:'/free-appraisal/'};
const brands=['Fender','Gibson','Les Paul','Martin','Gretsch','Rickenbacker','Other'].map(brand=>({brand,record:brand==='Les Paul'?lesPaulRecord:ranked.find(r=>r.brand===brand)!,href:brand==='Les Paul'?'/vintage-gibson-les-paul-market-value-guide/':brandLinks[brand]}));
---
<link rel="stylesheet" href={kitCss}/><link rel="stylesheet" href={recordsCss}/>
<article class="mg-guide mx-guide" data-auction-guide>
<header class="mg-hero"><div class="mg-wrap mg-hero-copy">
  <nav class="mg-breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/blog/">Blog</a><span>›</span><a href="/category/value-evaluation/">Value &amp; Evaluation</a></nav>
  <p class="mg-eyebrow">Auction Records · Updated {date(lastVerified)}</p>
  <h1>The Most Expensive Guitars Ever Sold</h1>
  <GuideActions brand="Fender" label="Sell Your Guitar" href="/"/>
  <p class="mg-dek">Verified million-dollar auction guitars, what an ordinary example of the same model is worth, and why the gap is so big.</p>
  <p class="mg-byline">By <a href="/about-me/">Joe Dampt</a> · Last Updated <time datetime={lastVerified}>{date(lastVerified,true)}</time></p>
  <div class="mg-hero-grid"><Photo photo={hero} width={1800} eager/><aside><b>At A Glance</b>
    <p><strong>{money(black.priceUsd)}</strong>Public Auction Record</p><p><strong>{money(acousticRecord.priceUsd)}</strong>Most Expensive Acoustic</p><p><strong>{biggestResale.multiple.toFixed(2)}x</strong>Biggest Verified Resale Multiple Here</p><p><strong>{ranked.length}</strong>Verified Million-Dollar Guitars</p>
  </aside></div>
</div></header>
<section class="mg-intro mg-wrap"><div class="mg-drop" data-prose><p>The most expensive guitar ever sold at a public auction is David Gilmour's {black.nickname}, which brought {money(black.priceUsd)} at {black.house} on {date(black.saleDate,true)}. That result tells you what a collector paid for a particular piece of music history. It doesn't tell you what another Stratocaster is worth.</p><p>This guide puts the auction results beside Joe's published model values and follows guitars that have sold more than once. The photographs show ordinary instruments from Joe's archive, with any difference in year or configuration stated in the caption. None depicts the famous auction guitar.</p></div><aside><b>How We Count</b><p>Public auction results include buyer's premium and use US dollars at the time of sale. Charity auctions are flagged. Private deals stay separate, and unverified candidates aren't ranked.</p></aside></section>
<nav class="mg-toc mg-wrap" aria-label="On This Page"><strong>On This Page</strong><div>{sections.map(s=><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.nav}</a>)}<a href="#faq">FAQ</a></div></nav>
{sections.map((s,i)=><section id={s.id} class:list={['mg-band',{'mg-dark':i%2===0}]}><div class="mg-section mg-wrap">
  <header class="mg-head"><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><div><p>Auction Records</p><h2>{s.title}</h2></div></header>
  <div data-prose><GuideBlock block={{type:'prose',html:s.html}}/></div>
  {s.id==='record'&&<div class="mg-stat"><b>{money(black.priceUsd)}</b><p>{black.nickname} · {date(black.saleDate,true)}</p><a href="https://www.christies.com/en/stories/jim-irsay-collection-results-483c129b13b54102a2207fbe89d236dd/">Christie's · The Jim Irsay Collection Results</a></div>}
  {s.id==='top-sales'&&<RecordsTable/>}
  {s.id==='ordinary-value'&&<><CompareCards/><p class="mg-prose" data-prose>Use the linked guide to check the assumptions behind each range. If your guitar doesn't fit its model, year, finish or condition, <a href="/free-appraisal/">ask Joe for a current appraisal</a> before using the comparison. The multiples describe the gap between two markets; they aren't a formula for valuing an unexamined instrument.</p></>}
  {s.id==='by-type'&&<div class="mx-type-grid">{(['electric','acoustic','bass'] as const).map(type=><div><h3>{titleCase(type)} Records</h3><ol>{(type==='bass'?bassHighs:ranked.filter(r=>r.type===type).slice(0,5)).map(r=><li>{r.nickname||r.guitar}<strong>{money(r.priceUsd)}</strong><small>{r.player}<br/>{r.house} · <time datetime={r.saleDate}>{date(r.saleDate)}</time>{r.saleType==='charity-auction'?' · Charity Auction':''}</small></li>)}</ol></div>)}</div>}
  {s.id==='by-brand'&&<div class="mx-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Auction Records By Brand"><table class="mx-brand-table"><caption>Highest Verified Result By Brand And Model Family</caption><thead><tr>{['Brand','Record Guitar','Price','Year','Joe’s Guide'].map(t=><th scope="col">{t}</th>)}</tr></thead><tbody>{brands.map(({brand,record:r,href})=><tr><th scope="row">{brand}</th><td>{r.nickname||r.guitar}<small>{r.player} · {r.house}</small></td><td>{money(r.priceUsd)}</td><td>{r.saleDate.slice(0,4)}</td><td><a href={href}>{brand==='Other'?'Ask Joe':`${brand} Values`}</a></td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>}
  {s.id==='sold-twice'&&<ResaleTable/>}
  {s.id==='no-famous-owner'&&<div class="mx-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Ordinary Vintage Guitar Values"><table><caption>Joe's Published Production-Model Ranges</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Joe's Range</th></tr></thead><tbody>{ordinaryModels.map(r=><tr><th scope="row"><a href={r.href}>{r.label}</a></th><td>{money(r.low)} to {money(r.high)}{r.plus?'+':''}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>}
  {s.id==='private-sales'&&<ul class="mg-prose">{privateSales.map(r=><li><strong>{r.guitar} · {r.player}.</strong> {r.reported} Reported by {r.reportedBy}.</li>)}</ul>}
  {s.id==='yours'&&<GuideBlock block={{type:'cta',title:'Get A Value For Your Guitar',html:'Send clear photographs and what you know about its history. Joe can assess the model, originality and condition before discussing a value.',appraisalLabel:'Free Appraisal',sellLabel:'Sell Your Guitar',sellHref:'/'}}/>}
</div></section>)}
<FAQ faqs={faqs} mainTitle="Questions About" subTitle="Guitar Auction Records" sectionId="faq" headingLevel="h2"/>
<section class="mg-closing mg-wrap"><div data-prose><p class="mg-eyebrow">The Last Word</p><h2>Keep The Guitar And Its Story Together</h2><p>A case full of receipts and old photographs can be more useful than a dramatic story with nothing behind it. Keep the documents, removed parts and repair notes with the guitar, and photograph them before anything gets separated. That gives the next owner a clearer account of what they're buying.</p><p>You don't need to prove a celebrity connection to ask for an appraisal. A well-preserved production guitar has a market of its own. Establish its identity and condition first, use the appropriate guide, and let the evidence decide whether a special history belongs in the valuation. The auction record can be interesting without becoming the asking price for every guitar that resembles it.</p><p class="mx-author-note">The guide ranges are estimates of market value, not offers to buy or guarantees. Market values fluctuate, and a hands-on inspection can change an assessment.</p></div></section>
<aside class="mg-author mg-wrap" aria-label="About The Author"><LicensedImage src={joePortrait} alt="Joe Dampt, owner of Joe's Vintage Guitars" width={140}/><div><p class="mg-eyebrow">About The Author</p><h2>Joe Dampt</h2><p>Joe owns Joe's Vintage Guitars. His model guides explain how identification, originality and condition affect a vintage guitar's value.</p><div><a href="/about-me/">Meet Joe</a><a href="/free-appraisal/">Free Appraisal</a></div></div></aside>
</article>
